{% set c = host.value.config ?? {} %}
<p>How this server introduces itself to other mail servers. Receivers check that these names, the IP address and its reverse DNS agree.</p>
<table class="kv dense"><tbody>
<tr><th scope="row">Mail host name (EHLO, PTR)</th><td><code>{{ c.POSTFIX_MYHOSTNAME ?? '?' }}</code> — Postfix greets other servers with this name; the PTR of the IP must be this name, and its A record must be this IP.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Public IPv4</th><td><code>{{ c.SMARTHOST_PUBLIC_IPV4 ?? '?' }}</code> — the sending address. catto-mail sends over IPv4 only.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Bounce domain</th><td><code>{{ c.SMARTHOST_BOUNCE_DOMAIN ?? '?' }}</code> — the envelope sender of every message (<code>bounce+&lt;token&gt;@…</code>); bounces and complaints return here. Its MX must point at the mail host name.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Sign-in mail sender</th><td><code>{{ c.APP_MAIL_FROM ?? '?' }}</code> — its domain needs a DKIM key too.</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p>Change them in <code>infra/.env</code> as for the web identity. The PTR is set at your VPS provider, not in your DNS zone.</p>
